.TH CHANPROBE 1 "November 2026" "chanprobe 0.3.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
chanprobe \- bidirectional audio channel prober for half duplex radio links
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chanprobe
.RB { initiator | responder | tune }
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chanprobe
measures the amplitude and phase response of a half duplex audio channel
(150\(en10500\ Hz) in both directions, using an extension of the ITU\-T V.34
line probing signal: 60 cosines on a 150\ Hz grid, with the V.34 Table 17 phases
up to 3750\ Hz and a crest\-factor\-optimised 0/180\(de pattern above.
Mute carriers (900, 1200, 1800, 2400, 3600, 4800, 6000, 7200, 8400, 9600\ Hz) give
the noise and distortion floor. As in V.34, the probe is sent at two levels:
L1 (peak \-3\ dBFS) then L2, exactly 6.02\ dB lower.
.PP
A session runs as follows:
.IP 1. 4
The responder waits for tone A (900\ Hz). The initiator sends it for 300\ ms.
.IP 2. 4
After t_w, the responder sends guard tone G (1200\ Hz, 200\ ms), probe L1 (1\ s),
G (200\ ms), probe L2 (1\ s), G (200\ ms). The initiator measures.
.IP 3. 4
After t_w, the initiator sends G, L1, G, L2, G. The responder measures.
.IP 4. 4
After t_w, the responder sends its measurements (one codec2 FreeDV DATAC1
burst: OFDM, about 1.7\ kHz wide around 1.5\ kHz, LDPC FEC, CRC\-16, about
4.4\ s), then the initiator sends its own.
.IP 5. 4
Both sides display the
.B Tx results
(measured by the remote side on the local signal) and the
.B Rx results
(measured locally on the remote signal).
.PP
Amplitudes are in dB relative to 1050\ Hz. The phase is the anomaly of each
carrier: received minus transmitted phase, minus the best straight\-line fit
across the band (bulk delay and constant offset, weighted by tone power), so it
is not referenced to any single carrier. The SNR is given per tone, in
its 150\ Hz bin. Silent bins show their level in dBc (re the 1050\ Hz tone).
The sample clock offset between the two sound interfaces is estimated and
compensated, and reported in ppm; the two sides may run at different sample
rates.
.PP
Each side also reports the received level of L1 (dBFS RMS; \-5.7\ dBFS RMS is
sent) and how long it heard each 200\ ms guard tone. A shortened trailing guard
tone means the end of the remote transmission is cut off, typically by audio
latency inside the radio that the sound card cannot see: raise
.B \-\-txtail
on that side. Each side also checks the continuity of the received probe: the lowest level of
any 6.67\ ms period (dropouts) and sudden timing steps between periods
(slipped or repeated samples, typically in a sound interface). With
.BR \-v ,
the timing of every transmission is logged.
.PP
Comparing L1 and L2 shows how the channel depends on level: for every carrier
the gain change beyond the 6.02\ dB step (compression) and the phase change
(AM/PM); for every mute carrier the distortion products found in its bin at
both levels, separated from the noise (the part that repeats with the probe),
and the order of that distortion, from how it scales (relative to the carriers
it rises by (n\-1)\ \(mu\ 6.02\ dB from L2 to L1). The overall level step from
L1 to L2 is reported too.
.PP
Levels: L1 peaks at \-3\ dBFS and L2 at \-9\ dBFS; A, G and the tune tone are at \-10\ dBFS, the
data burst at \-10\ dBFS RMS with peaks of at most \-3\ dBFS (a full\-scale
sine being 0\ dBFS).
.SH MODES
.TP
.B initiator
Start a session.
.TP
.B responder
Wait for an initiator and answer it. With
.BR \-\-loop ,
keep serving sessions until interrupted.
.TP
.B tune
Key the transmitter and send a 1\ kHz sine at \-10\ dBFS, to set the audio level
below the ALC threshold. Stops on Ctrl\-C or after
.BR \-\-duration .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Audio
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-device " \fINAME\fR"
ALSA device used for both playback and capture (default: default).
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " HZ"
Sample rate, 44100 or 48000 (default: 48000). Format is always S16_LE.
.TP
.BI \-\-channels " N"
Number of channels to open (default: 1). The signal is sent on all channels.
.TP
.BI \-\-channel " IDX"
Capture channel to analyse (default: 0).
.SS Push to talk (Hamlib)
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-model " \fIID\fR"
Hamlib rig model number (see
.BR "rigctl \-l" ).
Model 1 is the dummy rig; model 2 connects to a running
.BR rigctld (1).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rig\-file " \fIPATH\fR"
CAT port, for example /dev/ttyUSB0 (or host:port for model 2).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-serial\-speed " \fIBAUD\fR"
CAT serial speed.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-ptt\-type " \fITYPE\fR"
PTT method: RIG, DTR, RTS, CM108, GPIO, ... When given without
.BR \-m ,
the dummy rig is used, so that only the PTT line is driven.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-ptt\-file " \fIPATH\fR"
PTT port when it differs from the CAT port.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-set\-conf " \fIKEY=VALUE\fR"
Additional Hamlib configuration parameter (repeatable).
.PP
Without
.B \-m
and
.BR \-P ,
the transmitter is not keyed (VOX or bench use).
.SS Timing
.TP
.BI \-\-txdelay " MS"
Delay between keying the transmitter and the first meaningful sample
(default: 100).
.TP
.BI \-\-txtail " MS"
Delay between the last meaningful sample and unkeying (default: 30).
.TP
.BI \-\-tw " MS"
Turnaround wait t_w after the end of a received transmission (default: 100).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " \fIS\fR"
Receive timeout in seconds (default: 10).
.SS Other
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " FILE"
Also write the results to FILE.
.TP
.BI \-\-duration " S"
Tune mode: stop after S seconds.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More messages; twice for Hamlib debug output.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set the transmit level, PTT by CAT on an FT\-991A:
.PP
.nf
.RS
chanprobe tune \-m 1035 \-r /dev/ttyUSB0 \-s 38400 \-D plughw:CODEC
.RE
.fi
.PP
Responder at 44.1\ kHz, PTT on the RTS line of a serial adapter:
.PP
.nf
.RS
chanprobe responder \-\-rate 44100 \-P RTS \-p /dev/ttyUSB1 \-D hw:1,0 \-\-loop
.RE
.fi
.PP
Initiator, saving the results:
.PP
.nf
.RS
chanprobe initiator \-m 1035 \-r /dev/ttyUSB0 \-s 38400 \-D hw:1,0 \-\-csv run1.csv
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the session completed (results may be partial), 1 on failure,
2 on a usage error.
.SH NOTES
Each probe level is analysed over 128 periods (853\ ms) in its middle, which
leaves about 70\ ms at each end for squelch, AGC and filters to settle. Each
level starts at the end of the guard tone before it. The results message fits one DATAC1 frame (508 bytes); SNR, compression
and AM/PM travel with 0.5\ dB, 0.05\ dB and 0.5\(de resolution.
.PP
On SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP the transmitter is unkeyed before exiting.
